- How would you verify correctness for non-trivial patterns quickly?
- How does this approach scale if you extend to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to extract the odd bits instead of even bits?
- What are the trade-offs vs a loop-based approach on various architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to count 1-runs in a 64-bit word using bit-twiddling with no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Start mask n & ~(n << 1) identifies 1-run starts
- Parallel popcount technique to count bits
- Edge cases: all zeros, runs spanning MSB

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t countOneRuns64(uint64_t n){
  uint64_t starts = n & ~(n << 1);
  uint64_t x = starts;
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit operands?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based scan in terms of latency and pipeline efficiency.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive a compact, branch-free bit-twisting routine for combinatorial generation.

## Key Concepts

- Next permutation with fixed popcount via snoob
- Branchless 64-bit arithmetic and overflow behavior
- Edge-case handling in tests

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextWithSameBitCount(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
  ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
  return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for arbitrary precision (128-bit) using two limbs?
- How would you detect when no next permutation exists efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a branchless, efficient 128-bit bit-count using a proven 64-bit SWAR approach on two halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Treat 128-bit as two independent 64-bit parts
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// C-like helper (conceptual)
static inline uint8_t pop64(uint64_t v){
  v -= (v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
uint8_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses non-branching bit extraction (compression-like utility) crucial in low-latency paths. It tests SWAR techniques, mask reasoning, and edge-case handling for arbitrary masks.\n\n## Key Concepts
- SWAR bit-twiddling for packing bits\n- Mask-driven selection and order preservation\n- Handling 64-bit boundaries without loops\n\n## Code Example
```c
uint64_t compressBits64(uint64_t x, uint64_t mask) {
  x &= mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1)) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
  return x;
}
```

